00:00:02.899 --> 00:00:04.700 align:start position:0%
would you be more focused on a task if  you knew someone was watching you with a  critical eye what if that someone was a  friendly robot or a mean robot to test  the effect of robots on human  concentration researchers gave 58 people  a classic test to measure how much they  were paying attention when there was a  robot in the room participants were  shown a series of words in different  colors and asked to identify the color  in which the word was printed ignoring  the word itself more focus is needed  when the word itself is a color word  than when the word is color neutral the  difference in the time it takes to  identify both kinds of words is called  the Stroop factor the subjects took the  test twice first alone and then again  with a new set of words in the presence  of a robot which was programmed to turn  its head as if we were watching them  right before the color test the subjects  interacted with a robot asking in a  series of questions the robot was set to  either good mode giving friendly  positive responses or bad mode giving  abrasive negative responses subjects  completed the test fastest in the  presence of the bad robot it was faster  than when they were alone or observed by  a friendly robot previous research has  shown the presence of a belligerent  human can improve our focus on tasks  like the Stroop test but this was the  first time this was studied with robots  as social robots become a bigger part of  everyday life for things like elder care  and therapeutics it's important to  understand even the unintended effects  that robots might have on how humans act  and think so while researchers don't  think robots will end up bossing humans  around anytime soon being a little wary  of humanoid BOTS might actually be a  good thing when it comes to focusing on  the task at hand  [Music]  [Music]
 

